<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Miguel de Lillo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Miguel de Lillo is a church on Mount Naranco above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-asturias">Ramiro I of Asturias</a> around 842–850, near the hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-naranco">Santa María del Naranco</a>. Only part of the original building survives, with carved door jambs showing circus scenes copied from a Byzantine consular diptych. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of the Monuments of Oviedo and the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-lillo.jpg" alt="San Miguel de Lillo" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iglesia_de_San_Miguel_de_Lillo,_2021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
